Current xterms (XFree86 4.0 and up) can display Unicode by sending UTF-8 to 
them.  And I have yet to see a terminal program, at least one meant for 
Western languages, that can even begin to touch an xterm with the 
misc-fixed font when it comes to Unicode coverage.  Windoze comes off 
positively flat.  All of the Unicode 3.0 stuff in all five Latin-script 
blocks, IPA, Greek, Cyrillic including lots of weird archaic letters, 
dingbats a gogo, heck, it's even got runes, Armenian, Georgian in both the 
modern Mkhedruli and the archaic Asomtavruli scripts, and Ethiopic.  Of 
course the right-to-left scripts probably aren't useful for map symbols.

I had built a patch for an earlier Nethack that would take advantage of 
whatever Unicode features are available.  Print a mind flayer as U+0125 
LATIN SMALL LETTER H WITH CIRCUMFLEX and never confuse it with a dwarf king 
again.  Maybe it's time to update it and inflict it on the world.


